---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const winters = assertion('the-winters-warmed-and-the-summers-did-not')

const frost = assertion('the-frost-free-season-is-three-weeks-longer')

// Winter against summer, from the assertion's own two figures, so the chart cannot drift from the
// prose the gate checked.
const wintersSpec = bar(
  winters.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  winters.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Degrees Fahrenheit gained, 1895\u20131924 to 1996\u20132025', labelValues: true },
)

const frostSpec = bar(
  frost.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  frost.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Frost-free days', zeroBased: false },
)

// Lima against Van Wert, decade by decade. Van Wert is in another county and is here only to
// answer whether Lima's thermometer is measuring the climate or the city it stands in.
const frostDecades = [
  ['1900s', '156.8', '\u2014'],
  ['1910s', '162.4', '\u2014'],
  ['1920s', '159.4', '137.6'],
  ['1930s', '164.8', '156.7'],
  ['1940s', '153.8', '158.0'],
  ['1950s', '155.5', '154.8'],
  ['1960s', '154.8', '155.8'],
  ['1970s', '163.1', '162.3'],
  ['1980s', '167.6', '161.0'],
  ['1990s', '178.4', '183.6'],
  ['2000s', '179.1', '178.0'],
  ['2010s', '186.0', '181.5'],
  ['2020s', '182.5', '180.7'],
]

// The twelve months, both elements, at the two ends of the record. Typed out because the two
// columns come from two files and the point of the table is that they disagree in shape.
const monthChange = [
  ['January', '+1.19', '\u22120.15'],
  ['February', '+4.98', '+0.48'],
  ['March', '+2.18', '\u22120.52'],
  ['April', '+2.34', '+0.66'],
  ['May', '+1.30', '+1.14'],
  ['June', '+1.80', '+0.42'],
  ['July', '+0.22', '+0.27'],
  ['August', '+1.00', '+0.43'],
  ['September', '+1.39', '+0.35'],
  ['October', '+1.91', '+0.36'],
  ['November', '+2.25', '+0.11'],
  ['December', '+3.79', '+0.11'],
]

// Decade means, both elements, with the county's heating and cooling demand beside them.
const climateDecades = [
  ['1900s', '49.25', '35.21', '6,447', '754'],
  ['1910s', '49.44', '35.61', '6,398', '770'],
  ['1920s', '49.90', '36.86', '6,205', '732'],
  ['1930s', '51.22', '33.78', '5,963', '979'],
  ['1940s', '50.51', '34.86', '6,077', '836'],
  ['1950s', '50.28', '36.65', '6,128', '797'],
  ['1960s', '49.18', '31.73', '6,442', '712'],
  ['1970s', '49.56', '35.22', '6,358', '776'],
  ['1980s', '50.19', '35.07', '6,190', '831'],
  ['1990s', '50.87', '37.06', '5,888', '771'],
  ['2000s', '51.05', '38.54', '5,861', '811'],
  ['2010s', '51.60', '42.07', '5,788', '945'],
]
---

<Article slug="the-weather-the-farms-are-in">
  <p>
      Everything above is about ground. A hundred and thirty-one complete years of the sky over it
      — every month from January 1895, county by county, from NOAA's gridded climate record — make
      Allen County 2.03&nbsp;°F warmer than it was. Where that warmth went is the finding.
    </p>
    <Gloss>
      Nothing had been said here about the weather before this.
    </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={winters} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="winter-against-summer"
    title="Degrees gained, winter against summer"
    caption="December–January–February and June–July–August, comparing the thirty years 1895–1924 with the thirty years 1996–2025. The annual mean rose 2.03 °F and reports neither of these."
    columns={['Season', 'Degrees gained']}
    rows={winters.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value])}
    height={280}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="winter-against-summer" set:html={JSON.stringify(wintersSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The mean daily maximum of June, July and August was 82.48&nbsp;°F in 1895–1924 and is
    82.59&nbsp;°F in 1996–2025 — eleven hundredths of a degree in a century. The hottest summers
    here are still 1934 and 1936, and the county's cooling-degree-day record, set in 1934, has
    stood for ninety-two years. The warmest <em>years</em>, meanwhile, are nearly all recent: 2024
    is the warmest of the hundred and thirty-one, and eight of the ten warmest are since 1991. A
    record that puts its hottest summers in the 1930s and its warmest years in the 2020s is
    describing a winter.
  </p>
  <DataTable
    title="What each month gained, 1895–1924 against 1996–2025"
    caption="Temperature in degrees Fahrenheit, precipitation in inches. Nights warmed faster than days — the minimum rose 2.36 degrees against the maximum's 1.70 — and two months are drier than they were."
    columns={['Month', 'Temperature', 'Precipitation']}
    rows={monthChange}
  />
  <p>
    The rain went up 3.66 inches a year, and 3.27 of those inches fall between April and September
    — a sixth more water on the growing season than the county's farms had a century ago, at the
    same time as the frost-season warmth that lets a crop start earlier. March, meanwhile, is
    half an inch drier than it was.
  </p>
  <DataTable
    title="Allen County by decade, 1900s to 2010s"
    caption="The trend is not a line: the 1930s are as warm as the 2000s, the 1960s as cold as the 1900s and drier than any decade of the record, and the 2010s the wettest by three and a half inches. Heating demand is down a tenth since 1895–1924 and cooling demand up a seventh."
    columns={['Decade', 'Mean °F', 'Precipitation (in)', 'Heating °days', 'Cooling °days']}
    rows={climateDecades}
  />
  <p class="note">
    Two instruments that share no data agree about which years were wet here. The wettest month in
    the record is June 2015 at 13.48 inches and the second is August 2007 at 10.54 — and August
    2007 falls inside the ten-year hole in the Lima river gauge, one of three federal flood declarations the gauge is silent on. The gauge's own highest peak, 5,700
    cubic feet a second on 28 February 2011, sits in the fourth wettest February of the record and
    the wettest calendar year of it. The rainfall record cannot say what was in the river; it can
    say what fell on the ground, and it is not silent where the gauge is.
  </p>

  <h2>Three more weeks between the frosts</h2>
  <p>
      One station in Lima has kept a daily maximum, minimum, rainfall, snowfall and snow depth
      since 1 April 1901 — 45,601 days — and it answers a question a monthly mean cannot: how long
      the ground here goes without freezing.
    </p>
    <Gloss>
      Every other weather record read here is monthly, divisional or storm-driven.
    </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={frost} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="frost"
    title="Frost-free days a year, by decade"
    caption="Days between the last spring minimum at or below 32 °F and the first of autumn, at the Lima station. A year is counted only if it carries 340 daily minima; 117 of 125 do."
    columns={['Decade', 'Lima', 'Van Wert']}
    rows={frostDecades}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="frost" set:html={JSON.stringify(frostSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The station sits inside a city of thirty-five thousand, which is the one thing that could
    explain a warming measured entirely from night-time minima. It does not: Van Wert 1 S, in
    another county and outside a town a third of Lima's size, gives 181.4 days for 1996–2025
    against Lima's 181.0, and steps in the same decade. Two thermometers twenty-five miles apart
    agree on the modern figure to within half a day and on when it changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    At the other end of the year the same record says what the monthly one already said, by
    counting instead of averaging. The hottest day ever measured in Allen County is 109 °F on 14
    July 1936, and the twelve hottest fall in three summers — 1930, 1934 and 1936. Forty-five days
    in a hundred and twenty-five years reached 100 °F; twenty-nine of them are in the 1930s, four
    are after 1954, and none is after 2012. Nights at or below 0 °F have gone from 8.2 a year in
    the 1970s to 2.0 in the 2020s. The cold end of this county's climate has moved and the hot end
    has not.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    The same file finally separates snow from rain — 25.0 inches in the mean season, 58.0 in the
    snowiest and 2.0 in the least — and then declines to draw a trend through it. From the 1978–79
    season to 2005–06 the station reported snowfall on two days in twenty-eight winters while
    recording hundreds of days of precipitation falling at or below 34 °F. Those winters are not
    snowless; nobody measured, and the record wrote zeros instead of blanks. Thirty consecutive
    seasons drop out, which leaves the 1910s and the 2010s with a quarter-century missing between
    them, and a line through that is a selection rather than a series.
  </p>
</Article>
